The output assert statements would be as follows:
```cs

// @@@ If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in old array is smaller than or equal to new array
if (New_Ret != -1 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_Ret)
{
    Debug.Assert(true);
}
else
{
    Debug.Assert(false);
}

```

This assertion checks if the return value from the `IndexOf` method in the List is non-negative and if the last index of the element `x` in the old array is greater than or equal to the returned index. The debug assertion will pass if both conditions are true, otherwise it will fail. The `IndexOf` method returns -1 if the element is not found in the list. Therefore, the assertions ensure that the last occurrence of the index of the changed list is not lower than the return value from the test method.--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments in the given C# code.

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount >= Old_arrListCount);
```

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount || New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount || New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);
```

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 || (New_arrListCount - 1 <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX && New_Ret != New_arrListCount));
```--------------------Here is the c# code that uses Debug.Assert:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "If the return value is non-negative, last index of x in old array is smaller than or equal to new array");
```
The above csharp code uses Debug.Assert to verify that the new `IndexOf` value is greater than or equal to the last index of `x` in the original `arrList` otherwise, it will assert.--------------------```cs
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListCount || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListCount);
            // If the return value is negative, we assume New_arrListLastIndexOfX should not be equal or smaller than New_arrListCount
            // If we get a non-negative value, we check if the last index of x in old array is no more than the new array.
```

Here's the reason for the assertion: 

We are assuming that 'IndexOf' function will return -1 whenever 'x' is not in 'ArrayList'. In that case, the LastIndexOf(x) should also be -1. If 'x' exists in 'arrList' and the LastIndexOf(x) returns non-negative value, then the index could be between 0 and the last index where the element 'x' is found in the ArrayList. 

In the new array, if the element 'x' exists, the LastIndexOf(x) should also be found and should not be smaller than the size of the array (i.e, New_arrListCount). If the old and new arrays do not contain 'x', the assertions should still hold. But if 'x' is present in the old array, then in the new array, the last index should not be further than the size of the new array due to the modification to the list and also not smaller than its size.--------------------// @@@ The return value is non-negative, last index of x in old array is smaller than or equal to new array
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

// @@@ The count value should be the same in both old and new array
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ The item x should exist in both collections
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ If return value is non-negative, value should be less than or equal to count
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If return value is non-negative, x should be at the position returned by the index
if(New_Ret >= 0)
    Debug.Assert(Convert.ToInt32(PexObserve.GetValueForViewing("$new_arrList_ret")) == arrList[New_Ret]);--------------------```cs

// Add this line at the top of your Test file
using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? (Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX) : true, 
            "If return value is non-negative, last index of x in old array is not greater than or equal to new array");

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

